# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `docker compose up` refuses to start without
# these, which is the point: a server that boots with a signing key printed in
# its own repository is one that anyone who has read the repository can forge a
# session for.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored. Do not commit it.

# Signs session tokens, the key to every draft, and the token every form is
# handed out with so that its response is stored once. Anyone holding it can
# mint a session for any user, so generate it rather than inventing it:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#   # or, without openssl:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64'))"
#
# Changing it invalidates every existing session, which is also how you revoke
# them all at once — and every draft, and every form somebody is filling in:
# their response is refused once and has to be sent with a form read again.
FORMANCY_AUTH_SECRET=

# The first admin account, created ONLY while no user exists. It cannot re-seed
# an admin into a running installation, so changing these later does nothing —
# use the API to manage users after the first boot.
FORMANCY_ADMIN_EMAIL=
FORMANCY_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# The development database password. The database is bound to 127.0.0.1, so
# this is a weaker secret than the two above — but it is still a password, and
# a deployment that exposes Postgres beyond the host should set it.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=formancy

# Webhook delivery refuses plain http and private addresses, which is what
# stops a form author pointing the server at the cloud metadata service or at
# an internal admin panel. Both are opt-in, per deployment and never per form,
# because the form document is exactly the surface the guard defends against.
#
# Turn them on only for a receiver you run yourself on a trusted network — a
# sidecar on the same host, say. `ALLOW_PRIVATE` gives up the SSRF protection
# entirely for every webhook in the installation.
# FORMANCY_WEBHOOK_ALLOW_HTTP=false
# FORMANCY_WEBHOOK_ALLOW_PRIVATE=false

# Where uploaded files go. Unset means this deployment accepts none, which is a
# supported state rather than a misconfiguration: a form with a file field
# still renders and still submits, and the field says plainly that there is
# nowhere to put one.
#
# In a container this has to be a mounted volume, or the files disappear with
# the container. The server deliberately does not default it for that reason —
# a volume is the one thing a self-hoster has to think about.
#
# Both compose files do: left out of `.env`, it is their volume's mount point,
# and uploads are on. `FORMANCY_FILES_DIR=""` turns them off — the server reads
# an empty value as unset — and is what the object store below needs. Not with
# an image of v0.4.0 or older: those read the empty string as a path, the
# container's working directory, which the image's user cannot write — uploads
# are switched on into a directory that refuses them. Any other value is
# honoured too, and is a directory inside the container unless you mount a
# volume there as well.
#
# The local store does not survive more than one replica: two containers with
# two volumes each accept uploads the other cannot serve. Same constraint the
# outbox worker has, for a different reason.
# FORMANCY_FILES_DIR=/var/lib/formancy/files

# The operator's ceiling over every form's own limit, in bytes. A form author
# sets `maxFileSize` per field; this is the disk's owner having the last word.
# A whole number from 1 to 2147483647, the largest size the files table
# records; anything else and the server does not start.
# FORMANCY_MAX_FILE_BYTES=10485760

# An S3-compatible object store instead of a local directory, which is what a
# deployment with more than one replica needs: two containers with two volumes
# each accept uploads the other cannot serve.
#
# Setting the endpoint turns it on, and then the other four are REQUIRED -- none
# of them is defaulted, because a guessed bucket is a deployment that uploads
# into nothing and guessed credentials are a deployment where every file reads as
# missing. Setting this and FORMANCY_FILES_DIR together is refused at startup.
#
# Garage is what the design calls for and what the tests run against; the same
# settings serve MinIO, Backblaze B2, Cloudflare R2 and real S3. Addressing is
# path style, so no DNS per bucket is needed.
#
# The region is part of the request signature rather than decoration: a wrong one
# is a signature the store computes differently and rejects. Garage answers to
# whatever its own `s3_region` says, commonly `garage`.
#
# Through compose, blank FORMANCY_FILES_DIR as well, with the quotes: both compose
# files otherwise point it at their volume, and a server told about two stores
# refuses to start. The empty quotes are the value — not one left for you to
# fill in, as the two credentials are. It needs a server newer than v0.4.0:
# v0.4.0 and the images before it read the empty string as a directory.
# FORMANCY_FILES_DIR=""
# FORMANCY_S3_ENDPOINT=http://garage:3900
# FORMANCY_S3_BUCKET=formancy
# FORMANCY_S3_REGION=garage
# FORMANCY_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# FORMANCY_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# A ClamAV daemon asked about every upload before its bytes are kept. Unset means
# no file is scanned. Set, a file is kept only once clamd calls it clean, and is
# refused while clamd cannot be reached -- configuring a scanner is a promise that
# nothing unscanned gets in. Not checked at startup: clamd takes minutes to load
# its signatures. MAX_BYTES is clamd's own StreamMaxLength (100 MiB by default);
# a larger file is refused before it is sent, because past that limit clamd
# closes the connection mid-stream. Set AlertExceedsMax in clamd.conf as well:
# without it, content past clamd's MaxFileSize or MaxScanSize -- an archive that
# expands too far -- is answered as clean without being scanned.
# FORMANCY_CLAMD_HOST=clamav
# FORMANCY_CLAMD_PORT=3310
# FORMANCY_CLAMD_MAX_BYTES=104857600

# Turns the proof-of-work challenge on for anonymous submissions. At least 32
# characters. Unset means public forms are defended by the rate limits, the
# origin allowlist and the body cap alone — which is a supported state if no
# form of yours accepts a submission without a session, and thin if one does.
#
# Separate from FORMANCY_AUTH_SECRET on purpose: rotating this costs somebody
# an unsolved puzzle, rotating that costs everybody their session.
# FORMANCY_CHALLENGE_SECRET=

# A model for the builders, asked through this server so that its key stays here and
# never reaches a browser. The admin's prompt pane writes and changes forms with it, and
# its Translations tab asks it for a language's missing messages. Off unless set. Set,
# each such request sends the form -- its whole document, or its words -- to the provider
# named here, under that provider's terms, and the admin says which provider before
# anybody asks.
#
# All three or none. A key or a model without a provider, a provider other than these
# three, or a provider without its key or its model is refused at startup: a server that
# started anyway would have no model while you believed it had one. So is
# ANTHROPIC_CUSTOM_HEADERS or OPENAI_CUSTOM_HEADERS, whichever the provider's SDK reads
# (xai goes through OpenAI's): it would add headers to every request, the form's included.
#
# PROVIDER is anthropic (Claude), openai (ChatGPT's models) or xai (Grok). MODEL is the
# id as the provider's own documentation writes it, and there is no default: a default
# goes stale when the provider retires the model, and which model runs is what you pay
# for. claude-opus-5 below is Anthropic's Opus as its model list named it on 2026-06-24;
# check the list before copying it. With anthropic the model must have adaptive thinking:
# Claude Opus 4.6, Sonnet 4.6 or later.
#
# Every editor and admin can spend this key: ten requests a minute each, each answer up
# to 64,000 tokens. The audit log records who asked, for which kind of request, how long
# it was and how it ended -- never the text.
# FORMANCY_MODEL_PROVIDER=anthropic
# FORMANCY_MODEL_API_KEY=
# FORMANCY_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# The reverse proxy in front of the server, so the rate limits count the people
# filling in forms rather than the proxy. Behind one, every request arrives from
# the proxy's address, and until the server believes the X-Forwarded-For it adds,
# every respondent shares a single budget: thirty submissions a minute between
# everybody. Unset trusts no proxy, which is right when nothing is in front.
#
# An address, or a comma-separated list of addresses and CIDR ranges -- every
# proxy in the chain. A hop count, `true`, a name such as `uniquelocal` and a
# netmask are refused at startup.
#
# Name only proxies you run, and name the proxy's OWN address. Anything at a
# trusted address can write whatever client address it likes, so it chooses the
# address its limit counts -- a fresh one per request. Do not name the compose
# network's range: it holds the network's gateway, and Docker hands connections
# to a published port over from the gateway -- from this machine and from other
# containers, measured, and under Docker Desktop or rootless Docker perhaps from
# everywhere. Pin the proxy's address with `ipv4_address` and stop publishing
# 4380 when a proxy is in front; the self-hosting guide has the override file.
# Nor does this share a limit between replicas: each still keeps its own count.
# FORMANCY_TRUST_PROXY=172.30.0.10

# The server's log, on standard output: a JSON line for every request -- its method, its
# route as written (`/f/:path/submissions`, never the path that was asked for), its status,
# how long it took and its id, which every audit row the request wrote carries too -- and
# one for every error that answered a request, naming what was thrown by its kind and its
# code, never by its message. A request whose client left before its answer was sent is a
# `request.abandoned` line, with no status. No body, header, query string, answer,
# password, API key, draft key, email or file name is ever written: a line is built from a
# list of fields, and none of those is on it.
#
# info by default, which is every request. warn and error keep the requests answered with
# a 5xx, the errors that answered them and the listed events at that level -- not a 4xx,
# and not a client that left. debug and trace add nothing of the server's own. off keeps
# no log at all. Anything else stops the server at startup, so a typo cannot leave you
# without a log you believe you have.
# FORMANCY_LOG_LEVEL=info
